Dominic Raab: Government cannot apologize for decision to isolate Spain
In response to the government’s sudden decision to reimpose quarantine measures on Spain, Dominic Raab offered an explanation.
Ministers announced on Saturday that anyone who failed to return from Spain and its islands before midnight would be subject to mandatory self-isolation for 14 days.
Spain continues to be on the list of “travel corridors” for safe travel established by No. 10 Downing Street, but recent spikes in coronavirus cases have prompted public concerns about a second wave.
On Sunday, in response to the sudden reimposition of travel restrictions, Foreign Secretary made a defense that the move was a “live reaction” to the surge in infections reported there.
He said that on the previous day afternoon he had assessed the situation, “We made our decision quickly.”
He added, “We cannot apologize for this.”
"We must be able to take quick and decisive actions, particularly in relation to our own territory or Spain, and specific countries; we have to act."
Otherwise, Britain would face another risk of infection and trigger a second wave of the epidemic, followed by another lockdown.
The minister of transport faces mandatory quarantine like the holidaymakers heading to Spain.
After two consecutive days with over 900 new daily cases reported in Spain, the government made this decision.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists had already missed their chance to cancel or change their travel plans.
He said: "I know that for those in Spain or those who have been considering going to Spain, this decision is very unforgiving, but we must be able to take decisive action swiftly to protect Britain."
“We have made significant progress in controlling the number of cases and preventing another outbreak,” he said.
Shadow Health Secretary Jonathan Ashworth condemns the government’s approach as “completely haphazard.”
He said Downing Street’s sudden decision left holidaymakers “confused and disappointed.”
This Labour MP told Sophy Ridge that I understand why they made this decision, but they acted in an ill-considered fashion over the last 24 hours.
Those holidaymakers currently enjoying themselves in Spain will feel confused and frustrated that their employers will not accept two weeks of quarantine. Similarly those planning to go on vacation to destinations such as Tenerife would too. They do not know when they can come back to work.
"Government is just saying ‘We hope that employers will cooperate.’"
"They made this decision without any sense in the last 24 hours."
CDC says that Salmonella is a bacterial foodborne illness causing 135 million infections annually, 26,500 hospitalizations and 420 deaths.
Common symptoms of salmonella infection include diarrhea, fever, and abdominal cramps, which may appear within six to six days after exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms usually last from four to seven days; most people recover without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
A couple in California’s dog park was attacked during lunchtime because they were not wearing masks (video) - RT TV's US News
First came the mask insult; then came punches and kicks.
A couple from California found themselves in this situation when their dogs were having lunch at a dog park where one woman sprayed pepper spray at them for not wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley said they went to lunch at the Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego with their three-month-old Boston terrier on Thursday.
When two people take their masks off to eat and one "an old lady that popped out of nowhere" approaches them calling them "idiots" because they don't wear masks while saying, "We're not eating with our faces covered."
O'Brien forces the woman to leave the park and stop her harassing behavior, at which point she sprays pepper spray at both of them.
The video that bystanders took from where Kelley wrestled with the woman who was trying to take his bottle of hairspray eventually caused him to be sprayed.
After the attacker leaves with his dog, O'Brien can be heard crying in the background.
O'Brien uploaded the video to Facebook and told ABC 10 that he wanted this woman “to go to prison.”
California mandates masks statewide, even in fresh air places.
People can remove their masks during designated times, for instance when eating.
However, there is no mention in that state’s coronavirus pandemic guidelines of bystanders using non-lethal weapons to enforce this regulation.
Even in this “mask shame” era, it is still a crime and will be punished with a $1,000 fine or up to three years in state prison.
The boy from the Edo town who sold eggs refused to give bribes and was overturned; he received an apartment and free education of 100 rupees in exchange.
It is reported that Thursday, Paras Raykar's cart loaded with eggs was overturned by officials of Dharmapura Municipality.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who couldn’t sell eggs and had his van overturned by municipal officials went viral on social media, spreading to support from all over China two days later, helping his family recover losses and resume their business operations.
The boy’s family also said that politicians including Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had contacted them for help.
The boy's family also received a set of apartments and was promised to cover the children’s education expenses.
It was reported Thursday that a vendor selling eggs at Paras Raykar’s small cart was overturned by Idar municipal officials during the pandemic; in response, Idar has implemented the “from left to right” staggered opening measures.
In a video clip, the boy says that he was warned earlier in the morning by officials to either pay a Rs 100 bribe or have his car flipped over if he did not comply. This clip quickly went viral on social media.
The boy said that when he refused to hand over 100 rupees, the officials had his car overturned and smashed all the eggs in it.
The boy still says in the video that due to the pandemic, his daily sales of eggs have dropped, and he is even more devastated by the loss of these eggs.
Since Thursday, the boy’s family received extensive support from across the country, including cash and help in kind.
The locals also helped this family.
The Honorary Chief Minister, Digvijaya Singh, provided funding and assisted children in attending an institute; People's Party Member of Parliament, Ramesh Mendola, has assured that he will provide a flat for the family under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing programme.
The Indoors Club also provided rations and money to boys.
The offices of National Conference leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also came to his aid.
Paras' grandfather says: "We have received much support from people across the country."
People's Party Member Ramesh Mendola assures us that our apartments will be provided under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing plan.
He provided a bicycle and an installment of Rs 2,500.
Digvijaya Singh then provided Rs. 10,000 and promised to take care of two children’s education.
Rahul Gandhi's aides also called to help us.
(A representative from Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal’s office) also called and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also supported her.
Foreign Secretary Priti Patel demands explanation from Twitter and Instagram over improper content published by Wiley
Because Twitter and Instagram failed to promptly remove inappropriate content published by Wiley, Priti Patel is demanding an explanation from both platforms.
The police are now investigating allegations that Grime singer Rake has made a series of antisemitic comments on social media.
Minister of Interior Zhou said on Sunday that "the anti-Semitic content posted by Wiley is abysmally offensive."
“Twitter and Instagram failed to delete these content promptly; I have asked them for an adequate explanation.”
Social media companies must act more swiftly to remove such jaw-dropping hate speech from their platforms."
He criticized Twitter for ignoring antisemitism because of Wiley’s tweets that were still visible after being posted for twelve hours.
The site subsequently removed multiple tweets and temporarily banned the artist's account for seven days.
Twitter previously said it had suspended @wileyco "for violating our hate speech policy", while Facebook said that "Instagram does not allow for hateful content."
Saturday, the Metropolitan Police Department stated that it is “extremely concerned” about all reports of anti-Semitism and that relevant materials were being assessed.
Meanwhile, A List, Wiley’s management company, severed its relationship with the star.
The singer’s former manager, John Woolf, is also Jewish and wrote on Twitter that “Given Wiley’s recent anti-Semitic content, we @A_ListMGMT severed all ties with him today.” No space allowed here, please. This society has no place for antisemitism.
Earlier, he had said that both he and his colleagues would not support or encourage the comments of Wiley, but they would discuss them privately and “educate him.”
However, an Instagram video shows that Wiley appears to have severed ties with his management company.
Other members of Congress also criticized Twitter for not promptly removing the tweets.
Labour MP Jess Phillips tweeted on Saturday that she had just seen what Wiley had published. “Why should @twitter be allowing such blatant antisemitism and hatred content to persist? It involves all sorts of dangerous ideas – Jews get things you don’t, they’re in control, they think they’re superior, etc. Such stuff is very dangerous; it must be deleted.”
MP Neil Coyle adds that his agency seems to react faster than that of @Twitter, and I reiterate again what I previously said – we must ensure social media platforms are not used for spreading hatred through legislation (including the Online Harms Bill).
John Mann, an independent government advisor on anti-Semitism, called these posts “precisely what needs to be done for laws to change in order to fairly hold accountable those responsible.”
Cracking down on anti-Jewish movements requires police to investigate Wiley’s content and call for his social media accounts to be shut down “to prevent another wave of anti-Semitic venom from spewing out.”
Richard Cowie is also known as "the Father of Grime" and has received an MBE for his contributions to music in 2018.
Kudlow says next coronavirus aid package would have more stimulus checks and extend moratorium on evictions
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow on Sunday pledged that the federal government will extend the moratorium on evictions and issue another round of stimulus checks under the next coronavirus relief package.
Just weeks before Kudlow made his promise, the White House payroll tax cut was rejected by Congress.
Residents of Villages in Massart and Mallawi have refrained from making comments about that affair, except to offer consolation to parents of the children involved.
Khalid Darwish, an advisor to the National Council for Women and Children, said in October that about 93 percent of Egyptian children aged one to fourteen had been victims of violence.
He also noted that litigation is one of the major tools for eliminating child abuse and emphasizes that cooperation between the Committee and the prosecuting authorities in this regard must be enhanced.
A study conducted by the Committee on the Status of Women and Children found that children may be subjected to violence in places one would think should be quite safe - such as at home, school, or clubs – where they are supposed to be under the watchful eyes of people who were meant to protect them, such as parents or teachers.
The AP criticizes White House press secretary Kayleigh McEnany’s “politicized” briefing, but reporters keep asking political questions – RT America's US News
The AP accuses White House Communications Director Kayleigh McEnany of politicizing press briefings rather than keeping them news-oriented – and apparently forgetting that reporters choose which questions to ask.
The Associated Press published an article on Saturday calling President Donald Trump’s press conference “the most radical break from tradition in years,” as McEnany made it the venue for short and pointed attacks on the media rather than a more traditional opportunity to brief the president on what the government was doing – information that is relatively sparse.
The last evaluation has to be seen in context; after all, virtually no new information was revealed at the press conference.
The Associated Press didn’t mention that virtually nobody asked for any new information at press conferences.
Journalists routinely use White House press corps to draw attention to themselves – and CNN’s Jim Acosta takes this to extremes.
They transcended the common adversarial relationship between media and government – repeating over and over some of their most deceptive questions aimed at challenging President Donald Trump.
In some circumstances, they will make themselves news, for example, when CBS News Correspondent Weijia Jiang was asked to refute Trump’s “asked China first”, she made a less subtle implication: "Mr., why did you say that specifically to me?"
The July 6th event is an excellent example of its no longer being a source for Americans to access effective information.
On that morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR’s ratings had dropped because of false reporting on so-called hate crimes against driver Bubba Wallace and his decision to ban display of Confederate flags in races.
As had been expected, reporters one after another asked McEnany questions about this tweet relating to its support of the Confederate flag; they wanted her to state directly that she approved of Trump's endorsement of it.
McEnany did not let reporters get away with it; such an interpretation would damage Trump’s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president has no position on the confederate flag and that his tweets were about NASCAR fans being wrongly portrayed as racists.
The AP reports she sounded “flabbergasted” during the presentation.
The story of Reuters is in sync with the columnist article published on Capitol Hill earlier this month by Washington Post journalist Jonathan Karl, who also presides over the White House Correspondents Association.
Karl said that denying reality and using the White House podium for purely political purposes violates public trust.
McEnany succeeded Manafort as press secretary in April and chaired her first news conference on May 1, where her wit and combative style earned some Trump supporters to regard her as an hero.
The news secretary position had been vacant for more than a year before her appointment.
Like other aspects of Washington politics under Trump’s administration, the relationship between the White House press secretary and the media has changed dramatically.
This change has come about through both sides, long before McEnany's arrival.
In April of this year at a White House Correspondents’ Dinner hosted by President Trump, comedian Michelle Wolf questioned former press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders with such vicious insults that even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called it “shocking.”
Jill Colvin of the AP asked her at her first White House briefing on Monday whether she could pledge not to lie from that podium.
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume is a seasoned news person with more than 50 years of experience who served as ABC’s chief White House press secretary from 1989 to 1996 and said that today’s journalists exhibit “aggressive behavior” in news conferences because they don’t respect Trump.
He told Fox’s Tucker Carlson Tonight this week that he had shifted focus to opposing Trump and neglected policy news because journalists were chasing the anti-Trump angle.
“It is not just a game of cat and mouse,” Hume said in reference to media people’s stated objective.
Our purpose is not just to bring government officials down from their pedestals but to get information.
Californian protesters set fire to a courthouse.
Oakland (California) – Oakland police said Saturday that a peaceful demonstration in California escalated to a conflict event when protesters set fire to a courthouse and smashed police stations before attacking multiple officers.
Departments in Auckland posted to Twitter that protesters smashed windows, sprayed graffiti, and fired fireworks and used lasers on police.
Others on Twitter have called for peace; they wrote that “we ask organizers to help us provide safe spaces and locations for demonstrators.”
Protests began Saturday evening and were organized by organizations such as “Mother’s Wall” (similar to the groups in Portland, Oregon), among others. Meanwhile, protesters clashed with law enforcement officers who were deployed to protect the U.S. courthouse.
Since May 25, when a protest in Minneapolis ended with the death of George Floyd, US President Donald Trump has sent federal police to quell nightly protests.
At first, the demonstrators gathered peacefully in Auckland, but became violent at night.
Around midnight, police announced that the protest was an unlawful assembly and ordered the participants to disperse.
According to reports in the news, a fire broke out at the Alameda County Superior Court but was brought under control within a short time.
The police said that at one point protesters broke windows and made racial slurs against residents.
Departments associated with the event posted pictures of broken glass and graffitied walls at the police station on Twitter.
UN senior official cancels visit to Pakistan due to flight issue
ISLAMABAD (AP) -- The incoming United Nations General Assembly president has postponed her visit to Pakistan due to technical problems with flights.
In March last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir was elected as the Chairman of the 75th Session of the United Nations General Assembly, which was scheduled to arrive in Islamabad this Sunday.
However, he tweeted that plans to visit Pakistan's capital have been postponed.
Pakistan’s Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi tweeted in response to Bozkir that “We welcome your prompt visit to Pakistan and hope for a fruitful and constructive dialogue.”
Just one day earlier, Qureshi announced his trip enthusiastically and expressed his intention to discuss a range of issues with Bozkir, including Kashmir. Kashmir is located between Pakistan and its neighboring country India, where both claim sovereignty over the entire region.
Since gaining independence from British rule in 1947, Pakistan and India have fought three wars, of which two involved that disputed region.
The two sides have fired frequently in Kashmir, causing casualties among troops and civilians.
Pakistan hopes that the UN will adopt a resolution allowing people in the Himalayan region to decide whether they want to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for the settlement of the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United gets Champions League spot; Paul Pogba hints at staying with the club.
Having secured their place in the top-four after Red Bull’s victory, Manchester United star Paul Pogba hinted this summer he will stay with the team.
2-0 victory over Leicester means that Manchester United will finish third and ahead of Frank Lampard's Chelsea team.
In the 12 games preceding the final, Leicester was 14 points ahead of Manchester United but the Red Devils stormed to the semi-final.
In the last two years, Pogba has twice asked to leave the club and so fans have predicted that he will depart this summer.
However, the pandemic means there will be few teams willing to spend big on players this summer, and nobody can afford to pay both transfer fees and Pogba’s salary to bring him back from Old Trafford.
Such an explanation has been helpful in recent weeks as his performance and body language have improved.
The French player posted to Instagram after his team won 2-0 that he intends to stay with the squad.
Although Pogba may be merely celebrating his team's return to UEFA Champions League qualification, many fans understand it as an indication that he is strongly suggesting he will remain in the squad and take on top-tier European players again.
The deal has two years left, but the club can extend it for another year, which means he is effectively still serving his second-year contract.
Manchester United is keen to extend Pogba’s contract and wants to clarify his situation before the next season.
For Pogba, staying with the team is comfortable, and his arrival of Bruno Fernandes has made him stronger.
This Portuguese midfielder became Man United's heartbeat and his arrival allowed Pogba to push deeper in midfield where he could play more freely, and also improve his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Watford to relegation from the Premier League.
Some of Watford’s players knelt down and others left the pitch without turning around.
The whistle of Watford’s return to the Premier League for its fifth season has barely blown yet, but no one can say that Watford capitulated without a fight when Arsenal needed all their strength in chasing them down.
In fact, this was Watford’s most spirited and dynamic performance over the past five years, as they were just two goals behind Arsenal when their fight-back attempt almost succeeded in retaking the lead.
With just 16 minutes to go in the match, Danny Welbeck's backheel volley was saved by goalkeeper Emiliano Martínez, and had it not been for that save, Welbeck would have earned his third point for Watford, level with Arsenal and secure their place in the Premier League.
But Watford was not to his liking in the end, as caretaker manager Hayden Mullins suggested that if their previous form had been like this one then he would have been no more exhausted than he is now.
The Hornets have been sluggish through most of the season and the club’s management is in disarray as well under boss Pozzo.
A lost battle cannot destroy an empire, but the Pozzo family has not yet laid the foundation for Watford to win its way back to the Premier League permanently.
Watford's frequent change of managers has now become an inevitable fate.
On the whole, it will be eight years before a new manager is appointed for the club, but that fresh blood injected by change is very limited.
Troy Deeney faces relegation with his team, despite being able to continue playing in the UAE Super League.
Some players might opt to depart as well, for instance striker Ismaïla Sarr, who said that it seemed incredible to play in the Championship again in September, but saving strength is still the top priority for now.
Arsenal manager Mikel Arteta says that it is impossible to predict Arsenal’s chances in the clash, adding that this makes for an exciting encounter.
Arteta also suggested that the striker for Watford was “cheating,” but later clarified this with a football term of “fighting to stay in the relegation places.”
Arsenal’s statement is perhaps unsurprising that they want to go on and win – Arsenal already has the lead in both groups – so Watford have to press hard to get back in touch with their opponents.
Watford conceded a penalty just after the interval.
At 40 seconds, Craig Dawson inadvertently knocked Alexandre Lacazette to the ground during a kickoff, which was subsequently penalized after VAR verification lasted about three minutes, with Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang converting the penalty kick.
Watford pressurizes relentlessly, Sarr quickly counter-attacks with an initial pass from his right side; Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney provide cover in case Watford crosses the line.
A former Labor Party source told The Daily Mail that they would still sue even if Starmer ousted Corbyn from his party.
What I mean is that he really made the Labor Party competitive again.
He has much further to go on policy, but he is off to an impressive start – and has got there before many who have given up on the Labor Party entirely; those who were never Labor party voters at all now look like Laborites again and see him as a serious fellow.
Blair really does worry that Labour is still seen as just another tax party.
He said, "According to my experience, if you're a member of the Labor Party, then in tax matters, you have to be very careful because the public is not sitting there worrying whether or not you’re going to raise taxes; they are more likely worried that you will raise taxes, so you need to pay attention to this mindset."
The Coronavirus will permanently alter world economics (opinion)
Just as security measures have become ubiquitous after 9/11, virus screening could well be part of our lives.
It is imperative to make necessary investments in infrastructure to address future viral testing.
If SARS-CoV-2 immunity is only temporary, this investment can provide the guarantee for economic activities.
During the pandemic, many economies adopted policies akin to Germany’s Kurzarbeit subsidy scheme.
Under this policy, working hours and wages will be reduced accordingly, with any shortfall in wages to be subsidized by the government.
This step maintains the link between firms and workers, providing the foundation for robust economic recovery.
Improving policy implementation is also important; these policies need to be permanent components of our economic recovery toolkit.
Remote work is likely to become more common.
There is evidence that remote working can be just as effective as in-office work.
However, many companies remain reluctant to adopt remote working as a work mode.
Given that remote work has been widely tried and is showing good results, it may well continue.
With e-commerce further developing, telemedicine, video conferencing, online teaching, and fintech adoption has also seen an increasing uptake as well with the speed of digital transformation accelerated due to the COVID-19 pandemic.
Companies engaged in transnational supply chains are grappling with shortages and bottlenecks worldwide.
We may see many of these companies move production back to their home countries.
Finance Secretary Fiona Hyslop said that the pandemic had “crippled” all activities.
The restrictions put in place to prevent the spread of Covid-19 have affected sports events such as festivals and Highland Games in Scotland.
Ms. Hyslop said that while we have taken some positive steps, such as the resumption of car movie theaters, many industries will not be able to operate for an extended period.
We have closely and constructively collaborated with industry organizers on how they can safely run their businesses in response to challenges facing them individually.
A portion of the funding will also be used to promote innovation and support the long-term recovery of the events.
The Event Industry Advisory Group believes that the industry is one of the first to face lockdown restrictions and will be among those least able to fully lift them.
The group’s chairman Peter Duthie also holds an executive position at Glasgow Stock Exchange, where he has indicated that sporting events contribute nearly £5 billion to Scotland per year.
He says that it is necessary for the government to provide additional funding to this industry and has received widespread support from event organizers and numerous supply chain enterprises that have suffered serious economic losses over an extended period of lockdowns.
Shivraj Singh Chouhan is confirmed to have tested positive for coronavirus disease.
Chief Minister of Madhya Pradesh, Shri Shivraj Singh Chouhan confirmed on Saturday that he had tested positive for COVID-19 and said on his Twitter account later in the morning that he was "Fine" and praised the staff who have been working tirelessly at the forefront of the pandemic.
The Chief also reiterated his warnings from yesterday that people should heed public health guidelines such as maintaining social distancing and wearing masks.
“Friends, I’m fine. The heroes who fought against the pandemic risked their lives and gave freely without any expectation of reward; they all deserve praise. I salute all the frontline workers in Wuhan, China.” Chouhan tweeted from the Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal yesterday when he was admitted to the hospital.
"Keep two meters of distance, wash your hands frequently, wear masks – this is our most effective weapon against coronavirus," he said, "I hope everyone will use these weapons to protect themselves and their families."
Chouhan added, “Be careful. I hope all of you will be safe and healthy too. This is also what I pray for to God.”
Saturday, Chouhan tweeted that he had tested positive for the virus despite his best efforts to avoid such an occurrence; he warned that even a little bit of carelessness could lead to infection with the coronavirus.
Today early this morning, Chouhan tweeted that even with the threat of infection still present, there was “no need to be afraid,” and urged those exhibiting symptoms to go get tested.
He said, "If you have contracted the virus, there is no need to fear. Do not conceal your symptoms; immediately inform the doctor and start treatment. Timely treatment will help you recover."
The central states have nearly 27,000 confirmed cases, of whom 7,600 are active cases and there are 799 deaths due to COVID-19.
In recent weeks, India has seen an unprecedented spike in new cases, with daily case surges reaching over 45,000 since July 23rd.
The total number of confirmed cases of coronavirus is rapidly approaching one million. More than 467,000 were active cases while over 32,000 deaths were related to this virus.
So what would be more suitable for studying rocks on Mars than one from Mars? Prof. Caroline Smith, curator of meteorites at the Natural History Museum, puts it like this.
Smith is also a member of the “Mars 2020” science team and adds that scientists believe the rocks they returned were from Mars.
The tiny bubbles in meteorites have precisely the same composition as Martian atmospheric gases, so we can be certain this rock is from there.
It is believed that this meteorite was formed around 600,000 to 700,000 years ago as a fragment of a small asteroid or comet collided with Mars and scattered across space.
One pebble from it swept across the solar system and fell on Earth.
This meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been conserved at the Natural History Museum ever since.
Among the instruments aboard the "Perseverance" rover is a high-precision laser spectrometer called Sherloc that will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic molecules capable of indicating past or present life on Mars.
Adding another SAU 008 is to ensure that this operation is completed with maximum precision.
Smith adds that we specially chose this rock because it is chemically compatible with its surroundings and because it is a very hard rock.
"Some of our Martian meteorites were found to be very fragile; but this meteorite is hard like an old shoe."
Upon finding rocks of research value on Mars, "Perseverance" will cache them on the Martian surface.
Then these rocks will be sent to Mars rovers for collection and launched back towards Earth for research analysis.
Man shot in Mambourin near Verrières-le-Bailly
Last night, a man was shot in Melbourne's west and rushed to hospital for emergency treatment.
The police responded to an alarm at nearby Mambourin, near Weir Bay, at about 8 p.m., when they found a man of about 20 years of age, reportedly with multiple gunshot wounds to his lower body.
A man was taken to the hospital for treatment with no life-threatening injuries.
The police said they were investigating this case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I don't dress up on Sundays and just wear my pajamas."
Question: How did your Sunday start?
I stumbled down the stairs and was knocked over on my way to make tea for my wife.
After disembarking from the ferry, we drove up to the A3 motorway and headed back to London to prepare for the start of our workday. Upon arriving home, the children went to bed while I came back after walking my dog and had another bowl of cereal.
I am glad to go back to London from White Island knowing that I cannot have a home there otherwise.
Kenyan police officer arrested after gun attack in Garsenkwa
Two Kenyan police officers have been arrested over the shooting death of two people in a gun battle last week.
The shooting incident took place in the eastern Goris County market town of Soko Ng’ombe on Saturday when protests were taking place against the case of murder of a man.
The two victims were Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenya Police Inspectorate has launched an investigation into the shooting incident.
The probe includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Parliamentarian Aden Duale of Kaduna state called for "an outcry" over the incident and posted a Facebook statement condemning the “police brutality”.
Multiple activists voiced concerns about police conduct during the pandemic.
According to data from Amnesty International, Kenyan police have killed at least 21 people since March for failing to comply with curfew orders and wearing face masks in line with COVID-19 restrictions.
Earlier this month, police used tear gas and arrested members of a group that organized the annual Saba Saba march – an event marking a protest against President Daniel arap Moi’s dictatorship which had origins in a 1976 anti-apartheid demonstration in Nairobi.
A front reporter says that protests this year were more intense because of police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdown, which inflamed public anger.
Security Measures Enhanced at U.S. Consulate in Chengdu, China
Sunday, security was stepped up outside the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, and American personnel were preparing to leave.
Three moving-cargo trucks entered the museum; uniform police and plainclothes police lined up on either side of the streets, with metal barriers set up along the roadside.
China’s Global Times editor posted on Twitter that US Consulate in Chengdu was asked to close within 72 hours, i.e., Monday, July 27th at 10am (GMT 0200).
China accuses some personnel of the US consulate in Chengdu of “engaging in activities inconsistent with their identities.”
China’s Foreign Ministry spokesman Wang Wenbin did not divulge any details; instead he said that this activity interferes in China’s internal affairs and undermines its security interests.
China asked on Friday to close the US Consulate in Chengdu, as a retaliation for the US demand that its consulate be closed in Houston, Texas.
Washington wants China to close its consular office in Houston "to protect American intellectual property and personal information."
After closing its Houston consular mission on Friday, a group of what appeared to be American officials breached the premises through the back door.
Wang Wenbin said on Saturday that this behavior seriously violated international law and bilateral agreements and China will respond, but did not specify any measures.
It further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage as well as human rights issues by closing embassies in a confrontational manner.
The pranksters provide wrecking tools, and in the zoo, baboons wave all sorts of implements.
The primates at Newleigh Wildlife Park in Morecambe, Lancashire, are infamous for attacking cars parked inside their enclosure — smashing windscreen wipers or rear-view mirrors.
Someone saw apes at North Leigh Wildlife Park in Merseyside wielding knives, screwdrivers, and chainsaws.
Staffers fear that pranksters will provide these annoying primates with weapons and they will pounce on stopped cars, smashing windshield wipers or rear-view mirrors.
Now, staff fear that tourists may provide them with lethal weapons to enhance their aggression and have even seen these baboons rifling through toolboxes.
A park worker told The Sunday Times that “we’re not sure whether it was tourists who provided them with weapons to attack cars or they stole from pickup trucks and cargo vans.”
They do indeed go through other people’s toolboxes and carry them around everywhere. Someone has seen a baboon carrying a chainsaw.
Another staff member said, "We have seen baboons carrying knives and screwdrivers. I wonder if it's the tourists who give them these."
Lions attack frequently enough for anyone to know that they're in a zoo in England's northwest.
A mechanic from Greater Manchester’s Wild Animal Park says people know what to expect when animals go wild that there was one customer who lost both of their legs last year to a baboon.
They further said that just one minute before the children wanted monkeys to sit on top of the car and disappear the next minute when the license plate was missing. This kind of problem brought good business to repair shops.
Nossier Zoo insists that it is as safe here as McDonald's drive-through and will reopen on June 15 after taking precautionary measures to address the coronavirus pandemic, even though visitors can stay in their vehicles for the entire five-mile wildlife park tour.
The zoo occupies an area of about 222.7 hectares and houses lions, tigers, rhinos, wildebeest, and camels among others; they scoff at reports that baboons carry weapons.
A spokesman said that “we believe many of these stories have been exaggerated through word-of-mouth and have signs of embellishment, which sometimes makes it hard to believe what is found in the park.”
In 2012, the car manufacturer Hyundai placed its new i30 in an enclosure with 40 monkeys for ten hours to show that this model of vehicle is sufficiently robust to guarantee the safety of families with children.
Manchester United and Chelsea qualify for Champions League. Bournemouth and Watford slip out of Premier League.
Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea won against Leicester City at the end of the season in the Premier League to qualify for the Champions League while Barnsley and Watford were relegated after five years of dominance.
Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard scored as Manchester United won 2-0 over Leicester City to finish third in the league table under Ole Gunnar Solskjaer's charge.
Chelsea’s goal and assist from Mason Mount helped them beat Wolves 2-0 to finish fourth and overtake Leicester (which has been among the top four all season) for their last UEFA Champions League spot.
Leicester City will participate in Europa League competition as well as Tottenham which drew with Wolverhampton Wanderers at Crystal Palace Stadium (1-1). The latter has won due to an advantage over Wolves on goals difference.
The draw for the remaining two promotion places was pushed to the brink of injury time as Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute, then equalized just one minute later, and drew 1-1 with West Ham United.
Jack Grealish scores for Aston Villa and it might be his last game for his childhood club.
Bournemouth won 3-1 against Everton but ended up one point off Aston Villa.
Watford was relegated to League Two after losing 3-2 at Arsenal.
Manchester United scored more than 100 goals during their league season, defeating already relegated Norwich City 5-0 en route to securing qualification for the Champions League. This was David Silva’s final appearance in the Premier League for Manchester United after 10 years of service with the club.
Kevin De Bruyne breaks records with 20 assists and two goals in the season so far.
Liverpool beat Newcastle United 3-1 and ended its title hopes this season; the club finished with 99 points.
Moreover, Southampton defeated Sheffield United 3-1 and Danny Ings netted his 22nd goal of the season.
And Leicester City player Jamie Vardy has scored the most goals and won the Golden Boot award.
Gwyneth Paltrow hopes her children will have good “values”.
Gwyneth Paltrow says she tried “not just to hand them fish but teach them how to fish,” and wants her children to have good values.
Actress Gwyneth Paltrow has two children with her ex-husband Chris Martin, both of whom are teenagers, aged 16 and 14 respectively, Apple and Moses; she hopes they will cultivate good values rather than rely on their parents' fame.
“I also think it’s important to cultivate children’s professional ethics because… I believe that having their values aligned with your own or with how they see themselves is very important,” she told Rob Lowe on the podcast.
The truth of the matter is that I can point you in the right direction, but if you just try to do things like others, nothing will come of it.
She adds: "As parents, I can do nothing more for my children than to teach them right from wrong and what work is." I have tried hard not just to give them fish.
Kardashians also take steps to keep children’s lives as private as possible and to ensure their social media accounts remain private.
Given the upcoming elections, the Renewal Party eventually agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz says Pelosi intends to "solve problems with money and make America default."
WASHINGTON — Texas Republican Senator Ted Cruz lashed at House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and the Democrats for pushing through their coronavirus relief package, calling it “an attempt to use money to solve problems that don’t exist and shut down America,” while ongoing discussions of subsequent measures continue.
On Sunday, when she appeared on Face the Nation for an interview, Cruz said the United States faced two crises simultaneously – that of a global pandemic that took more than 600, 000 lives worldwide and economic collapse “of absolute magnitude.”
Cruz criticized Pelosi for coming to his aid before she was interviewed on "Face the Nation."
Cruz said that her objective is to use money as an instrument for solving problems and causing America to shut down.
“Especially you should take note of the $3 trillion bill she is trying to push through; it’s simply throwing money around rather than actually solving anything. Our objective should be getting Americans back to work and providing them with a decent living.”
Senate Republicans and the White House are expected to have hammered out details of a $1 trillion-plus coronavirus response plan by last week's talks on the fourth phase of the crisis.
Last weekend, White House Chief of Staff Mark Meadows and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Capitol Hill for further discussions with Senate staff members, where Mnuchin told Fox News on Sunday that Majority Leader Mitch McConnell will reveal the details on Monday.
The most pressing issue facing parliamentarians and a key point of contention between Republicans and Democrats is that unemployment benefits have expired and will be cut off this weekend.
The Democrats want to keep additional $600 in aid that was added during the “fourth phase” of the coronavirus relief package while Republicans are pushing for its reduction, arguing that some Americans earn more than their wages from unemployment benefits.
Cruz says that half of Americans now receiving higher unemployment benefits earn more than they do while employed and notes he learned this firsthand speaking with small business owners in Texas who have found it difficult to fill jobs because some workers want the higher benefits.
He said that they of course would not come back to work because their federal government pays them as much as two times what they earn in wages.
Cruz believes Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill that would ease taxes and regulations for small businesses so Americans can return to work.
He said: “The recovery bill is not to throw millions of dollars at it; rather, it suspends payroll taxes, which means that every working American gets a raise. This is how we can get people back to work.”
President Trump last week demanded that any future aid package include wage-tax cuts, but Mnuchin said it will not be included in legislation because Republicans do not accept the proposal.
President Trump confirmed that the next bill would not include a payroll-tax cut but aimed to pin the blame on Democrats.
Cruz has objected to the cost of this plan last week and on Sunday said he disagreed with the measure proposed by the Senate Republican Party.
But he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed in May by the House of Representatives controlled by Democrats for being deliberately crafted to harm the president’s interests before an election in November.
He said that “the Democrats’ sole objective is to defeat Donald Trump; they naively believe that the best way to defeat him is to shut down every aspect of American life and close every school in America.”
PM Narendra Modi pays tribute to Kargil heroes, says Pakistan trying to “stab India from behind”
Today is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, and Prime Minister Narendra Modi in his tribute to the martyrs of the armed forces said that while India has been trying hard to maintain friendly relations with Pakistan, it still tries to stab India from behind.
Prime Minister Modi says in his monthly radio address, "It is Pakistan that has caused this calamity; they devised malign plans to grab India's land and divert its internal conflicts."
He said that Pakistan is shameless in attempting to occupy Indian territory and divert public attention from its own internal upheavals.
At the time, India was working to repair its relationship with Pakistan.
But, as it is said, the essence of evil is to fight with everyone for no reason.
Such people with such fundamental character would do evil even to those who want to help them.
He says, "That's why Pakistan stabbed India in its back after it worked so hard to shore up their relationship."
He added: “Imagine what it would be like to have your troops fighting on top of high mountains while enemies were entrenched below.” But with our high morale and real power, we won against the mountain-based enemy forces.
"Our valiant armed forces have displayed tremendous strength in this Kargil campaign," Mr. Modi said, recalling his observations during an official visit to the battlefield.
India holds an annual celebration to commemorate its victory at Kargil, when the Indian army recaptured all of its posts on Ladakh Territory that had been seized by Pakistani troops over 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army announced that "Operation Vijay" was concluded successfully.
The war that lasted nearly three months in high altitude regions of Ladakh has claimed over 500 Indian soldiers' lives.
Earlier this morning, senior officials and army leaders paid their respects to the martyrs at the Amar Jawan Jyoti monument at the National War Memorial, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of the Defence Staff Bipin Rawat, Army Chief M M Naravane, Air Chief Marshal R K S Bhadauria, and Admiral Karambil Singh.
Relations between India and Pakistan have been tense for decades after the Kargil War, marked by a series of terrorist attacks.
India insists that talks and terrorist attacks cannot happen simultaneously; Pakistan needs to dismantle all terror camps active on its soil.
Lender firms warn SME loan scheme may not achieve its goals.
Small businesses rarely get big banks to lend them money, and hence increasingly turn to peer-to-peer lenders to satisfy their funding needs.
However, Mr. Callaghan said that, unlike larger banks, online lenders do not have an advantage because they cannot obtain the same cheap financing at all. He also warned against SME guarantee schemes.
He said: "I think this scheme may not achieve its objective; people phone me with the expectation that I will provide very cheap funding rather than half of the government loan."
So they felt very disappointed after we called them because someone wanted to get a discount price while our financing costs had not changed at all.
Callaghan added that if fintech firms were able to obtain cheaper funding and pass on those funds to small businesses, this would be “a cinch.”
"Getting in and out of this situation is a bit difficult – large banks can obtain funding at a lower cost, but they would prefer to avoid risks, so actually they don't lend funds in the same way, and unless you have collateral, they won't feel secure accessing information, " he said, "unless we can get cheaper funding, I think that people will be fantasizing about how popular such a proposal will be."
Moula in Melbourne also participated in the design of the scheme, CEO Aris Allegos said that government-suggested guarantees of 50 percent should be handed over to small businesses, but different lending institutions would take different approaches.
He said that "I can understand because others may not have experienced this cost structure."
It may relate to their capital structure, or whether they lend to which categories of borrowers and how they assess the risk in those categories.
Mr. Allegos would not reveal exactly how much money was to be disbursed under Moela’s scheme, but he said that revisions of the proposal “concentrate on highlighting changes required,” thereby improving flexibility for both lenders and borrowers.
But he said that he was very concerned about the “dual-speed economy” now in operation and its impact on small businesses.
He adds that for small businesses, one of the biggest problems facing Moula and lenders alike is the cliff, how to manage it.
“In sum, although demand is growing somewhat, it has not yet returned to pre-COVID levels.”
Prospa, an Australian securities exchange-listed peer-to-peer lender, is unwilling to disclose the amount of loans it has made under the scheme, which it calls commercial sensitive information; however, CEO Greg Moshal said that the government’s extension of the scheme was well received.
He said, "We support all enhancements to this scheme that allow small businesses to access a wide range of funding products meeting their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products."
Virginia Beach demolishes Southern Alliance monument
Virginia Beach (VA) – Sunday, Virginia Beach City Council demolished a 115-year-old Southern Alliance monument from public property. The city council unanimously approved its removal two days ago.
It was reported that workers demolished this 27-foot (8.2-meter) tall monument on Saturday morning.
The monument stands outside the old court of Anne Princess County in 1905 and slave auctions were held here.
Thursday night, the city council unanimously approved the related demolition work.
The city council heard the views of dozens of speakers who said the monument meant division to many and reminded them constantly of painful memories.
Given recent protests and vandalism at other monuments, this one has been completely covered up, with locked gates surrounding it.
The monument will be temporarily preserved.
The city is now waiting for responses from historical organizations or other relevant entities that are willing to deal with this monument.
Protests continue; police try new low-lethal tools
Violence between police and protesters in Portland and around the country continues as arguments rage over how to reduce lethal force used by authorities.
Some companies are ramping up efforts to provide police with new high-tech tools such as remote-controlled handcuffs and stun guns to replace pepper spray, rubber bullets, and flashbangs – weapons that are more dangerous.
Amnesty International analyzed 500 videos of protests from May 26 to June 5 and identified 125 instances in which force was disproportionately used across four U.S. states and the District of Columbia.
Brian Castner of Amnesty International said that they had seen situations where tear gas, pepper spray, pepper balls, and 40-millimeter impact grenades were used inappropriately, mostly as combinations of low-lethal weapons.
A portion of people still persist in this view that having selective enforcement is important for avoiding use of firearms.
Corey Pegues from former New York Police Department’s Deputy Inspector stated that we ought to provide various personal protective equipment to policemen rather than using lethal weapons like bullets because policeman is the only profession in America where citizens can use their rights to take human life.
There is a new tool called Bola Wrap, a handheld device that launches an 8-foot Kevlar rope to wrap around a suspect.
Other new types of equipment include long-range acoustic devices (also known as sonic cannons), directed energy weapons that heat up human skin with laser beams, and new kinds of artillery shells made from materials such as foams and chalk dust, as well as stink bombs and electric stun guns.
Days after George Floyd’s killing by police in May, protests turned violent and Axon, the maker of the taser that killed him, saw its stock price surge more than 18%, reaching an all-time high.
The market for low-kill weapons is expected to reach $11.85 billion by 2023.
Watch the video to learn about police's use of new high-tech low-lethality tools, how they are being regulated, and what risks their deployment poses for future public order maintenance and community safety.
UN says thousands of Pakistan-based insurgent militants are stationed in Afghanistan
Islamabad – The UN report says that more than 6,000 Pakistani insurgents hide in Afghanistan, most of them illegal members of the so-called Pakistan Taliban, responsible for attacks on military and civilian targets in Pakistan.
According to the report released this week, the group known as Tehreek-e-Taliban (TTP) is linked to the Afghan branch of the Islamic State (IS).
Some of the TTP’s members have even joined an organization located in eastern Afghanistan that is aligned with ISIS.
Sunday, the Afghan government did not reply to The Associated Press’s request for comment on such issues.
Reports claim that IS (al-Qaeda's affiliate in Helmand Province), which has been heavily hit by the Afghan security forces and allied US and NATO troops, as well as occasional attacks from the Taliban in Afghanistan.
The report was written by the support and monitoring group for analysis and sanctions, which is responsible for tracking terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that ISIS members in Afghanistan number around 2,200, although their leadership has weakened, they still regard one Syrian citizen, Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani, as a leader among them.
The report mentions that members of the monitoring team were reportedly informed that two senior al-Qaeda commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, had arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report states that “the territory controlled by IS is decreasing but they remain capable of carrying out attacks in various parts of the country, including Kandahar.”
The organization also wants to entice opposition to signing with the US on an agreement with Afghan Taliban fighters”. Here, the agreement refers to the peace deal signed between Afghanistan and the Taliban in February by the United States.
The purpose of this agreement is for the US to end its 19-year war in Afghanistan and for the Taliban to guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
The deal is expected to ensure the Taliban fully commit to the fight against ISIS.
The second part of the agreement – which is also arguably the most important part – calls for talks between Taliban and Kabul political figures.
On Saturday afternoon, US State Department issued a statement saying that its envoy for peace Zalmay Khalilzad continues his busy efforts to push forward negotiations over prisoner release plans that have been repeatedly delayed owing to disagreements between both sides.
The US-backed accord demands that the Afghan government release 5,000 Taliban prisoners in exchange for the Taliban’s willingness to set free 1,000 government and military personnel as part of preliminary negotiations.
Currently, the Afghan government refuses to release about 600 Taliban prisoners and calls them high-profile criminals, but proposes to release only the remaining ones.
The Taliban rejected the government’s offer.
This is an opportunity of the greatest historical significance for both parties to engage in internal negotiations and an essential next step on the path to end the 40-year Afghanistan war, as expressed by the State Department.
“Although progress has been made in prisoner exchanges, further efforts are needed to resolve the issue.”
This week, in advance of the Id al-Adha holiday (an Islamic festival), the Taliban’s spokesperson stated that it was planning to hold talks with political leaders in Kabul after releasing prisoners at the end of the month, provided that this was done first.
The most worrying issue for Pakistan is the armed elements in Afghanistan, especially those linked to groups such as TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, Lashkar-e-Islam or the Balochistan Liberation Army; they carried out large-scale attacks on southern Sindh and southwestern Balochistan provinces this month.
Multiple Pakistani soldiers have been killed in clashes with militants in southwestern Balochistan province.
The TTP carried out one of their most horrific attacks in Pakistan in 2014 that killed 140 members of the Pakistani Army Academy.
Most of them were students and some were as young as five years old.
The report states that “foreign terrorist fighters operating in Afghanistan pose a threat to the security of two countries, with an estimated number between 6,000 and 6,500 people most of whom belong to al-Qaeda.”
HSBC denies setting up traps for Huawei in America's investigations – RT Business News
The multinational bank HSBC based in London said on social media that it had not fabricated evidence against Huawei as was reported in Chinese media, leading to the arrest of Huawei’s chief financial officer in Canada.
A now-irrelevant WeChat article states that banks said their actions had not triggered the US to investigate this global telecommunications equipment supplier for allegedly violating US sanctions against Iran because an investigation into whether Huawei was in violation of US sanctions had already begun before the bank’s intervention in late 2016.
Bank said on Saturday that HSBC had no hostile intent toward Huawei “and did not have any information regarding ‘tainting’ of Huawei”, and added it would never misrepresent any facts pertaining to its customers.
In response to the US Department of Justice’s request for information, HSBC merely stated objective facts.
HSBC did not “fabricate” evidence or “cover up” facts.”
The platform has shielded this article and is indicating that it "violates regulations." The article content cannot be viewed currently.
After multiple Chinese media outlets (including those with government ties) accused HSBC of colluding with Washington to set up a trap for Huawei, HSBC quickly issued this explanation.
In December 2018, Canadian authorities arrested Huawei CFO Meng Wanzhou at US Department of Justice request. During the past one and a half years, this company has been the focus of diplomatic news.
Ms. Meng faces fraud charges for allegedly using shell companies to violate sanctions against Iran and concealing Huawei’s relationship with Iranian company Skycom from banks.
The new evidence cited in The Global Times shows that HSBC was very aware of the relationship between this Chinese company and Skycom.
It is reported that the bank chose to collude with US authorities in order to evade more severe penalties imposed on it in 2012, when HSBC was forced to admit wrongdoing related to money laundering and sanctions violations, including restrictions on Iran.
The bank was ordered to pay out around $1. 9 billion in penalties and to take steps to prevent similar wrongdoing from occurring again.
The paper in question states that “there is evidence to suggest that HSBC knew about this relationship and deliberately sold its customers’ confidential business documents to the US authorities with the aim of allowing the US government to overlook the bank’s own improper conduct and avoid the penalties it should have been subject to under the 2012 Deferred Prosecution Agreement.”
The newspaper, under state control in China, adds in an open statement that if banks are found to have “collusion” with each other, they may face “severe punishment” (in Hong Kong, one of their main markets) if proven correct.
In recent weeks this well-known bank has come under repeated criticism.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting implementation of the new national security law in Hong Kong, which is its business headquarters and contributes about half of its profits there.
Hsbc is in a double-bind situation that leaves it at odds with both the West and East: Secretary of State Mike Pompeo calls it “kowtowing to the United States,” while China’s media call for its support as “latecomer.”
Angola: Pedalé Hospital Transferred to Health Ministry Oversight
Luanda - Presidente do MPLA João Lourenço has decided this week that he will transfer the oversight of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital currently under construction in Luanda to the Ministry of Health.
The statement released by the Office of the President of the Republic of China states that such a move is aimed at implementing government construction principles and expanding hospital networks so as to ensure better medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document notes that in this unique situation, such measures have enabled Angolan people to obtain one differentiated and valuable hospital equipped with modern technology and diagnostic and treatment instruments for complex diseases.
The statement in the article emphasizes that President of Angola’s decision also takes account of population growth, which is propelling “a huge demand” for the most diversified quality health services.
The need for such resources is reasonable, as hospitals encompass the treatment of complex diseases related to ophthalmology and maternal and child health, which should be within the responsibility of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghamama -- Algeria lost a patriot and a steadfast fighter.
Algiers —– President of Algeria Abdelmadjid Tebboune expresses his condolences to National Assembly Member and patriot Mujahid Mahmoud Guemama who died on Friday and mourns the loss of someone devoted to the great cause of people and country in Algiers.
Tebboutine wrote in his condolence letter that “Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama has departed; Algeria lost an patriot who had devoted himself to the cause of the people and the country from the moment he picked up arms, never wavering in his resolve, steadfast in his determination, and fighting alongside the valiant mujahideen brothers until he took his last breath on this day we mourn with such sorrow, and can only listen to God’s will.”
Mahmoud Guemama, who has passed away—May he rest in peace—was born to an exceptionally lucky generation of patriots willing to sacrifice themselves for their country.
In 1960, they joined the Tahart training camp of Wilaya VI at their most brilliant period and marched in line with the Revolution; freeing Algeria from the grasp of the vile colonialism; giving their lives for it.
The late leader possessed tenacity and devoted himself to building a united and prosperous Algeria; his name is now well-known throughout the country.
The President added: “In the distant south, Mahmoud Guemama is revered as a mediator who steps in to help his people reach reconciliation when the sparks of discord ignite.”
These qualities and values have earned Mahmoud Gueïma the respect and trust of the Tamanrasset people, who have elected him to represent them in multiple terms in the National Assembly as their spokesman on issues that matter and their defenders, and also their representative for all Algerian citizens.
No tribute can do justice to Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace.
Today he left us when Algeria needed him most., said the President.
Finally, Teboulle said that "I pray to Almighty God. May the departed be bathed in your radiant light of holiness and mercy, for they will greet him in infinity with the company of martyrs and sages."
I pray that true God will bestow upon his family and loved ones patience and comfort... Only you have infinite power."
Coronavirus Travel Guide: Countries Restricting Entry for Bulgarian Passengers
Many countries are implementing travel restrictions. From this week on, the Czech Republic wants to test Bulgarian seasonal workers for COVID-19. Denmark stipulates that only Bulgarian citizens who have registered their address in its territory, hold an employment contract or a business travel document can enter the country.
Starting today, anyone who has visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days must be quarantined in Italy under mandatory conditions. Finland only accepts travelers with “legitimate reasons” to enter.
Austria requires mandatory quarantine despite a negative PCR test result; Germany also applies such policies to incoming travelers. In Romania's southern region for the next fourteen days, no one may enter without sufficient reason. Greece has taken stricter measures at its land border.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that from next Tuesday, arriving persons from Bulgaria to Greece must present negative PCR test results issued before their entry into the Greek southern neighbor within 72 hours of their arrival in Greece.
They also announced that travelers must have corresponding test certificates to be allowed entry; these documents must be issued in English and include three names and numbers from the passport or ID of the traveler. If they do not meet the above requirements, authorities in neighboring countries will not accept them.
Passengers must undergo nasopharyngeal swab testing at the extraction port/lab to complete an RT-PCR test in a laboratory. The accepted results include those obtained from tests conducted on samples taken from national reference laboratories, national health laboratories, or private labs that have been certified by relevant national accreditation bodies (not necessarily dedicated to testing for the novel coronavirus).
Greeks, their resident citizens in Greece, and those who have significant professional reasons are exempt from this measure.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that arriving passengers on July 28 (Tuesday) and July 29 (Wednesday), if they have not been tested before departure, will be tested upon arrival in Greece.
The following requirements remain in effect: All travelers arriving by plane to Greece must complete an electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF), generate a QR code and present it at Greek authorities upon arrival in the country. For more information, see travel.gov.gr.
At this time, there is no change to the requirement for travelers crossing between land borders of Bulgaria and Greece. The Foreign Ministry explained that “travelers who cross into Greece through its land border regardless of their nationalities will be required at least 24 hours prior to arrival to fill out an electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF), which generates QR codes.”
The closure of the Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvodovo and Makaza checkpoints has caused an increase in traffic along the border near Petrich. There is no issue with overland crossings, nor any queueing for trucks. These people are waiting to go out to sea from Greece. Travelers have to wait about one hour and a half to pass through.
There are three checkpoints operating along the Bulgarian-Greek border. Only "Ilinden - Exohi" and Ioannina segment is currently closed.
Dominic Cummings visits SAS base but is held hostage
The head of state’s chief advisor had a taste of being held hostage last week during a visit to the base.
For some harassed civil servants,挟持Dominic Cummings by pointing an AK-47 at his head as hostage might be rather attractive.
The attitude toughened prime ministerial chief adviser’s visit to the SAS headquarters at the Sterland Line last week was no doubt an experience of a lifetime for him.
The Sunday Mail has revealed that part of his Wednesday itinerary includes visiting an "indoor anti-terror training facility" where elite soldiers hone their skills to combat terrorists.
He still guided a tour of the Herford base and inspected the military equipment of the Legion; he heard briefings on recent operations, including efforts to eliminate Al-Quaida’s Islamic terrorists from Iraq.
The important visitors to the Stirling line-up were specially welcomed – try out “Inside Counter-Terrorism Training Centre”, where SAS trains close-quarter fighting in this two-storey building.
Princess Diana had visited here as well during an exercise in 1983 when her hair was singed by flares from an accidentally thrown hand grenade.
It is known that Cummings arrived at the base in a helicopter named after its founder, Sir David Stirling.
He also visited nearby Polnica Training Area, where other secret units were stationed.
This visit was one of several stops on his tour of Britain’s highly classified security facilities, which drew attention from political and military leaders alike.
When news of its plans was leaked earlier in the month, Conservative backbencher and chairman of the House of Commons Defence Select Committee, Tobias Ellwood, said that MPs should have the same opportunities as ministers and civil servants to ask him about defence and security matters.
The Special Committee has rarely seen a situation in which politicians have appointed special advisors to give testimony.
It is understood that senior officials from SAS highlighted last Wednesday’s events for their significance in maintaining personnel numbers of both the Royal Marines and Special Air Service (SAS) Parachute Regiment.
It has reportedly been reported that these units, which have sent in large numbers of personnel to support Special Forces units, might be scaled down or merged in order to save costs.
A defense source says that SAS consists of around 2,500 soldiers, with half previously serving as paratroopers or as Special Boat Team (SBS) personnel. The smaller scale of SBS without support from Royal Marines could have made it difficult to continue its existence.
"Cummings was informed that he must preserve these units in their entirety."
Mr. Cummings has complained that military procurement is continuing to squander billions of pounds. He recently attended a meeting held at the headquarters of MI5 and MI6, and there is also a plan to visit the main SBS office in Porthleven, Cornwall, as well as the National Secret Laboratory in Broadweymouth, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of National Defense did not respond to a request for comment at night last night.
"Late Sunday Morning" Daytime Special: A Tribute to Rodgers and Hart
It marks the centenary of the debut performance of one of Broadway's most legendary songwriting duos, Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart.
To honor these two songwriters, Broadway stars Jelani Alladin (of "Frozen" and "Godzilla") and Christiani Pitts (of "The King's Speech" and "The Four Kings") as well as dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (of "I Love Mike" and "Shuffle Along"), have performed a modern style version of "The Lovers," specially recorded for viewers of "Sunday Morning."
Bassist Russell Hall and music director/arranger Bryan Carter were responsible for the musical direction and arrangement of the performance, alongside them was drummer John O’Connor.
The California wildfires in Lassen County are under control.
Lassen County, northeast of Sacramento in California’s San Joaquin Valley, is currently battling two wildfires that have consumed over 12,000 hectares.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection said Saturday morning that the "Gold" Fire in the south of Arden Hills had been brought under control to only about 40 percent, with many houses, barns, and ranches still threatened by the wildfire, which could have a significant impact on local agriculture, livestock production, and timber operations.
The fire has been burning for four days and has destroyed about 849.8 hectares of land and seven buildings, with two firefighters injured and taken to the hospital.
Firefighters worked late into the night to construct and reinforce firebreaks under limited conditions.
But according to weather forecasts, it was high winds and low humidity that day.
The Hog wildfire that broke out in the western part of Susanville has destroyed two buildings and about 4,047 hectares (over 10,026 acres) of land.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection said that after firefighters worked overnight to secure and reinforce the fire line, the flames have been contained.
As of Saturday morning, the fire had been burning for six days and 45 percent of it was under control.
Be wary of those charming gardening "tricks" that seem harmless at first glance.
To plant enthusiasts this might be an embarrassing thing indeed, but I am quite addicted to garden tricks.
Thinking on the bright side, I consider myself open-minded or maybe even irrationally optimistic, and many aspects of the natural world – in particular plants – cannot easily be judged through intuition.
But I am not ashamed to admit that I have fallen prey to such horticultural tricks time and again.
While I fully believe in the value of trying new things, if one is not passionate about opening up the “Hellish Mode”, it might be advisable to avoid some of the most common pitfalls found in gardeners’ schemes.
First of all, there is undoubtedly the biggest risk zone - grafting tomatoes to potatoes.
Planting tomato plants on top of potatoes has been hailed as an revolutionary breakthrough; in fact, it was something people tried at least two decades ago.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family and have roughly similar internal “pipes,” so they could indeed be cross-bred for enhanced magic.
But the problem is that if you create resource-intensive structures like fruit and root crops, you need to consume vast amounts of energy just to metabolize them. Planting two different kinds simultaneously requires allocating their limited resources.
This means that these grafted plants yield very low yields and differ entirely from what is being promoted in the pictures you show me.
Besides, different crops ripen at different times, so potatoes have to be harvested before tomatoes turn ripe.
At the same time, the publicity images also contain many deceptive ploys.
I've seen roses processed in Photoshop with all their petals turned to shades of lavender, and some fruit trees produced so many fruits that they were unbelievable.
Some merchants have even “borrowed” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim that they are trial sites on online shopping websites.
This is the behavior of some unscrupulous merchants, which forces you to view these advertising images with distrust, particularly those originating from the net.
If it looks like too good to be true, then it probably is.
When it comes to blue roses, there is relatively little of it in plants, so if you see new varieties of the colorful haworthia, orchids, or cacti glowing with a bluish light under high-concentration “disinfectant,” they are almost certainly sprayed or dyed.
If a supplier specifies something clearly, there’s not much to it really, but they rarely do.
Blue phalaenopsis revert to white if they lose their chlorophyll (as in the case of many orchids), but succulents and yucca-like cacti coated with an inhibitor dye will die soon after exposure.
Finally, I must expose the shame of bonsai kits that promise miniature trees and worse bonsai seeds.
Bonsai is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Potted plants are generally mature shrubs that have been trained to grow in a particular direction using lines before they are potted.
I know that I will continue to be fascinated by these horticultural gimmicks and that this is part of experimentation’s fun.
But if you sidestep these issues, you may be able to get an item that matches what the seller has shown.
Spanish quarantine measures: Do you need to isolate in Spain?
No need, but if you return from Spain after midnight tonight, you must quarantine yourself at home for 14 days.
Justice Secretary Humza Yousaf said this week that no restrictions were to be imposed on returning Britons from Spain because there was evidence of an improvement in the spread of the virus.
But clearly, the latest data warrants concern and necessitates overturning our original decision.
We know it will be disappointing to do so, but we have always been clear that we will closely monitor any outbreaks of virus in different countries and that should such an outbreak reemerge, we may ask for its removal from the list of countries not subject to mandatory lockdown measures.
"The virus is still rampant and lethal. Our priority must be to control the virus, prevent its spread, and protect public health."
The government has not yet announced regulations regarding British citizens traveling to Spain, but you should check local rules in your area if you plan on visiting Spain.
Spanish emergency health coordinator MarÃa Jose Sierra said:
"There is a major epidemic here. This might be the second wave. We need to pay attention to what happens in the next few weeks."
Pakistan team Imam-Ul-Haq injured during warm-up can still participate
The Pakistani and England teams will face each other in three Test matches and three T20Is.
Imam-ul-Haq of Pakistan’s cricket team is to be considered fit after he was struck in the left hand during an intramural warm-up match at Derby.
The Pakistan Cricket Board (PCB) stated in a statement that "a right-handed cricket player who is accustomed to using his left hand attended a warm-up match before the start of the next day's matches."
Imam was hit on the left hand while playing against White team PCB's Naseem Shah at Day One of the competition.
On Friday evening, when Imam was injured and forced to retire after being struck in the left hand by a ball thrown by Shah, he participated in an early morning warm-up match but will return to the field on the third day.
More than 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have tested positive for COVID-19.
Officials from the government department of the Tiruchirappalli municipal corporation in Tamil Nadu and one state-owned bank said that at least 38 employees of a main branch of the bank located in Tiruchirappalli had tested positive for coronavirus.
Officials said that they had suggested to customers of the bank who visited it voluntarily take virus tests.
Previously, an executive at this branch had other complications and died of COVID-19.
Officials say they have done a mass medical screening of the branch recently and discovered these positive cases.
The branch manager said that they had completed disinfection and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officials are in touch with customers who visited the bank.
Tamil Nadu is the second-most affected state in India with 206,737 cases as of Saturday. Maharashtra has reported the highest number of infections at over 1 million.
India's Prime Minister to attend inauguration of temple at disputed site
NEW DELHI (AP) -- The Hindu temple being constructed at a site in northern India has been razed to the ground, according to a trust overseeing construction of temples, and Prime Minister Narendra Modi is expected to attend its inauguration ceremony next month on a disputed spot in northern India where it will replace an 18th-century mosque that was bulldozed by Hindu extremists in 1992.
The ceremony is scheduled to take place on August 5 and organizers say that this day is auspicious for Hindus but also marks one year since the Indian Parliament revoked the semi-autonomous status of Jammu and Kashmir, which has a majority Muslim population.
The supporters and opponents of the Indian nationalist Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), which has long pledged to wrest Kashmir’s autonomy from Delhi, will miss this symbolic day. The party’s manifesto has always promised to build at the original site of the Mughal mosque where Rama was worshipped before his birthplace in Ayodhya is built. It is believed that Ayodhya, which lies in Uttar Pradesh’s Ayothia district, is where Rama was born.
The coronavirus remains rampant throughout India, as reported to be the third-most-affected country globally. Given this situation, and in light of its alliance with the Indian National Congress, the Vishwa Hindu Parishad, an Hindu nationalist group that is allied with Indian people's parties, has claimed that the ceremony will be broadcast live on national television and that participation would be limited to those who could attend it from their homes.
The spokesperson for this organization, Vinod Bansal, stated on a Saturday news conference that the temple will serve as “a center of everlasting social harmony, ethnic unity and fusion,” awakening Hinduism’s spirit.
Following the People’s Party’s overwhelming victory in last year’s elections, a century-long dispute over that site is now settled.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of temple trusts, saying it would allocate five acres elsewhere to Muslim petitioners.
For decades Hindu zealots have claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of temples in the ancient city of Aizawl.
The riots that followed the destruction of the mosque in December 1992 were sparked by community violence and resulted in the deaths of approximately 2,000 people, most of whom were Muslims.
Meanwhile, the special court is still adjudicating on demolition cases at mosques.
An architect from Ahmedabad, home to Modi's hometown of Gujarat, proposes building a stupa-like sandstone structure with five domes at 161 feet (49 meters) tall.
